      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gail, Texas takes its name from Gail Borden Jr., the inventor of condensed milk, honoring his legacy in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gail%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1891, Gail became the county seat of Borden County and has held that role ever since.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gail%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1939 Borden County Courthouse, a single‑story brick building with cast‑concrete details, stands as a local landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gail%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A historic marker placed outside the old jail in 1967 commemorates Gail’s early law‑making days.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gail%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Gail sits at the junction of U.S. Route 180 and Farm to Market Road 669, a crossroads of the Permian Basin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
